    "case_id": "46",
    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old male reported experiencing gynaecomastia, initially observing a swelling in the left breast two decades ago. This swelling had progressively enlarged over time. After a recent period of weight loss, the swelling became more noticeable. Physical examination revealed a 4 × 4 cm firm mass located directly beneath the left nipple, which appeared to be attached to the skin. There was no attachment to the underlying muscle and no lymph node enlargement was detected. Additionally, there were no signs of neurofibromatosis, including the absence of café au lait spots.\n\nMammograms showed a well-defined mass measuring 36 mm in its maximum diameter and immediately adjacent to the left nipple. Fine needle aspiration cytology showed stromal fragments containing spindle cells suggesting a soft tissue lesion of neural origin. Core needle biopsy revealed a spindle cell infiltrate. The spindle cells had irregular nuclei, many expressing S-100 protein. No mitoses were seen.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
